 To tackle this problem, we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers. We want to maximize the function $f = a\sqrt{b}\sqrt[3]{c}\sqrt[4]{d}$ subject to the constraint $g = 36a + 4b + 4c + 3d - 25 = 0$. We introduce a Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$ and form the Lagrangian $L = f - \lambda g$. The partial derivatives of $L$ with respect to $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, and $\lambda$ will be set to zero.

\begin{align*} \frac{\partial L}{\partial a} &= \frac{\partial f}{\partial a} - \lambda \frac{\partial g}{\partial a} = \sqrt{b}\sqrt[3]{c}\sqrt[4]{d} - 36\lambda = 0 \\ \frac{\partial L}{\partial b} &= \frac{1}{2}a\frac{1}{\sqrt{b}}\sqrt[3]{c}\sqrt[4]{d} - 4\lambda = 0 \\ \frac{\partial L}{\partial c} &= \frac{1}{3}a\sqrt{b}\frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{c}}\sqrt[4]{d} - 4\lambda = 0 \\ \frac{\partial L}{\partial d} &= \frac{1}{4}a\sqrt{b}\sqrt[3]{c}\frac{1}{\sqrt[4]{d}} - 3\lambda = 0 \\ \frac{\partial L}{\partial \lambda} &= g = 36a + 4b + 4c + 3d - 25 = 0 \end{align*}

Solving this system of equations, we find that $a = \frac{5}{36}$, $b = \frac{5}{4}$, $c = \frac{5}{4}$, and $d = \frac{5}{3}$. Plugging these values back into the function $f$, we get the maximum value as $\boxed{\frac{5}{12}}$. </think> <answer> $\frac{5}{12}$ </answer>
